  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 63-year-old man with hypertension and CAD underwent CABG two months ago and developed postoperative delirium. Today, despite orientation to person, place, and time and a normal neurologic exam in clinic, he continues to endorse intermittent confusion, delusions, and paranoid thinking. A noncontrast head CT last month showed no acute infarct. The patient has no prior history of dementia or psychiatric illness. What is the next best step to evaluate this persistent cognitive deficit?",
    "recommendations": "Although the CT was unremarkable, persistent postoperative neurocognitive dysfunction merits a brain MRI without gadolinium to identify subtle infarcts, microhemorrhages, or structural lesions. Additionally, a basic work-up (CBC, CMP, TSH, and vitamin B12/folate) can be performed to exclude metabolic and nutritional contributors, and MoCA can objectively quantify cognitive deficits. Review and reconcile all current medications for agents that may contribute to cognitive dysfunction (opioids, sedatives, and anticholinergics). Continue psychiatric follow-up for delirium management. If MRI findings are normal but the agitation and cognitive symptoms persist, arrange a neurology consultation for advanced evaluation such as EEG or neuropsychological testing."
  },
